Rounded corners, snapping and windows that draw their own title bar.

The rest of the LikeOS window frame for ModernTitles (0003), and what an
application needs to draw its own title row (LikeOS Explorer puts its tabs
there).

1. Rounded corners.  A window's frame is shaped (X Shape extension) to a
   rectangle with 10-pixel corners while it is not maximised, snapped or
   fullscreen.  The one-pixel border is the frame's X border on the straight
   edges; along the curves it is drawn by four small child windows of the
   frame, stacked over the title bar and the application, shaped to exactly
   the pixels of the curve and with an empty input shape, so clicks go
   through them.  Against the title bar the curve is anti-aliased (the
   caption buttons are inset, so the curve always meets the title bar's own
   colour); against the application's own pixels it is a crisp line.
   It takes the border colour of the focus state, like the straight edges.
   The cut-off corner pixels show the invisible resize border beneath the
   frame, and a press there resizes from that corner.

2. Maximised windows show no edge.  fullzoom() places a maximised frame with
   its border just outside the work area and the shape takes the border
   away, so the caption and the contents reach the screen's edges.

3. Caption buttons act on release.  Minimise, maximise and close show a
   pressed state (a deeper wash, close a darker red) while held and act when
   the button is let go over them; moving off first cancels, and so does
   pressing a second mouse button.  The function then runs as from a menu
   entry, with no button held any more -- f.delete and f.destroy otherwise
   wait for the release of the button they think is still down.

4. Snapping.  A window dragged by its caption (or by _NET_WM_MOVERESIZE, or
   with Alt) to the top edge of a monitor is maximised when it is put down;
   to the left or right edge it fills that half of the monitor's work area,
   and counts as zoomed (F_LEFTZOOM / F_RIGHTZOOM, _NET_WM_STATE_MAXIMIZED_VERT)
   with square corners and the maximise glyph.  While the pointer is at such
   an edge a thin rounded outline in the highlight blue (2 pixels, radius
   12, 8 pixels inside the area) shows where the window will go.
   Dragging a maximised or snapped window restores it to its normal size
   under the pointer, which keeps its place along the caption in proportion,
   and the drag goes on; restoring later puts it back where it was before it
   was snapped.  A window that may not be maximised, a dock, the desktop and
   ctwm's own windows do not snap.  The size and position box is not shown
   during moves and resizes with ModernTitles: the window itself shows both.

5. Applications that draw their own title row.  One that asks for a
   border only through _MOTIF_WM_HINTS gets the border, the rounded corners
   and the resize band, and drives the rest itself:
   - _NET_WM_MOVERESIZE with a SIZE_* direction now resizes that edge or
     corner right away, as a drag on the window's edge does (ctwm waited for
     the pointer to cross a border first);
   - a move or resize starts from the press position in the message rather
     than where the pointer is when it arrives, so a move started after the
     drag threshold does not leave the window trailing the pointer;
   - _NET_WM_STATE_FOCUSED is set on the window that has the focus and
     advertised in _NET_SUPPORTED.  Without it GDK takes every window to be
     focused, so GTK never drew a window in its backdrop state and a
     client-drawn title bar could not show itself inactive.

